          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "Because your anxiety symptoms have been persistent and are interfering with daily life, it is important to seek medical advice to address these ongoing issues.\n\nAnxiety is typically assessed using standardized questionnaires like the Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) questionnaire, which measures severity, as well as through discussions about your thoughts and feelings related to anxiety (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder\" and \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\"). Regular follow-ups can determine the ongoing impact of your symptoms.\n\nThe first-line treatments recommended for anxiety include Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and pharmacotherapy, specifically selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) or serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\"). Physical activity and relaxation techniques are also suggested as adjunctive treatments (source: \"Physical activity and anxiety\").\n\nIn the ICD-10-CM classification, anxiety disorders are classified under the code range F40-F48, covering various conditions including generalized anxiety disorder and panic disorder (source: \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 5: mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders (F01-F99)\"). Discussing your specific condition and treatment options with a healthcare provider could be very beneficial.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
